  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the evolution of display technology from early rigid screens to recent flexible textile displays, pairing each milestone with a simplified working principle. A horizontal timeline highlights four stages: cathode ray tube displays in 1938, liquid-crystal displays in 1988, organic light-emitting diode displays in 2006, and textile displays in 2021.\n\nBelow the timeline, each technology is illustrated by its basic mechanism. CRTs use an electron beam emitted from a cathode and steered by deflection plates toward a fluorescent screen to generate light. LCDs are shown as layered structures in which background light passes through polarizing filters, a liquid-crystal layer, and a color filter to modulate the transmitted image. OLEDs are depicted as stacked thin films between an anode and cathode, including hole and electron transport layers around a light-emitting layer, where injected charges recombine to emit photons. The textile display concept extends this idea into woven fibers, with crossing threads acting as electrodes and a light-emitting region at their junction, suggesting a screen integrated directly into fabric.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes a progression toward thinner, more flexible, and more structurally integrated display platforms.",
